# `@fc/ui` — design system

Presentational components only. They take data and callbacks; they do not know
where data comes from or where a click leads.

## Forbidden here

- `next/navigation`, `next/router`, `next/link` — a component that navigates
  belongs in `apps/web`
- `fetch`, any API client, any store
- Business logic. Formatting is fine (`formatMoney`); deciding a discount is not
- Imports from `@/` (the app)
- Hardcoded colours. Use tokens: `var(--fc-text)`, `var(--fc-surface)`,
  `brand-600`, `danger-500`. A hex literal in a component is a review failure

ESLint enforces the first and fourth of these (`apps/web/eslint.config.mjs`).

## Accessibility is part of the component, not a later pass

Every interactive component ships with:
- an accessible name — visible `<label>`, or `aria-label` when the control is
  icon-only
- keyboard operation, including Enter/Space where a native element would have it
- a visible focus state (the global `:focus-visible` ring, or a `has-[...]`
  variant when the real input is transparent)
- state exposed to assistive tech: `aria-invalid`, `aria-busy`, `aria-current`,
  `aria-describedby` wired to the error element
- decorative SVGs marked `aria-hidden="true"` with `focusable="false"`

Two patterns already settled, follow them rather than reinventing:
- **Loading buttons** use `aria-busy` + `aria-disabled`, not the `disabled`
  attribute, so the element keeps focus and the state change is announced.
- **Visually hidden inputs** are transparent and full-size
  (`absolute inset-0 opacity-0`), never `sr-only`. A clipped 1px control cannot
  be clicked by a pointer or by Playwright.

## Shape of a component

```tsx
export interface ThingProps {
  value: Value;
  onChange: (next: Value) => void;
  className?: string;
}

export function Thing({ value, onChange, className }: ThingProps) { ... }
```

- Named exports only, re-exported from `src/index.ts` along with their prop type.
- `className` last in the `clsx()` call so callers can override.
- Polymorphic `as` props are restricted to a small union typed against
  `HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>`; full generic polymorphism leaks into every call
  site (see the `Card` comment).
